% What a record costs against what banding a page saves.
%
% Holding a page's marks instead of its pixels buys the raster the page
% does not have to hold, and that raster is the whole of what it buys:
% the run less one band of it, because a page held in bands still holds a
% band. What the marks cost follows the drawing and has no bound at all,
% so a page can be drawn whose record costs more than the raster it is
% saving -- and that page is held in bands anyway and pays both.
%
% This is the page that does it, and this run is where the two sides are
% read off the interpreter and put where a wrapper can compare them.
% Nothing here decides anything: what is printed is a measurement of a
% page that came out right, on both of the routes a page can take.
%
% Two shapes, chosen by BIG:
%
%   0   a page a little past the band budget, so it bands into two and
%       saves one band's worth of rows and no more. That is the shape a
%       run meets by accident rather than a trick -- every page a little
%       too large for the budget is one -- and it makes a drawing worth
%       more than the saving cheap to write, so the boundary can be
%       asked from both sides for the price of a few hundred marks. Its
%       height follows the colour space because what a row costs does,
%       read off the device, which answers on both routes.
%
%   1   the page the cost was measured on: a full sheet at print
%       resolution carrying a drawing of small filled rectangles, which
%       is the ordinary content that costs a record most. Its saving is
%       the larger part of the page and its record is several times
%       that, which is the case in full rather than at the boundary.
%
% M says how much is drawn. The two shapes spend it differently -- a
% scatter over the small page, a grid over the large one -- and both come
% to M marks of the kind a record holds most cheaply, so what the record
% costs is the drawing and not one awkward mark.

/num { 20 string cvs } bind def
/ID 1183615869 internaldict def

/M where { pop }{ /M 50 def } ifelse
/BIG where { pop }{ /BIG 0 def } ifelse

% The record device, where the run reached one. Every reading below is
% asked of it rather than of the page: a page put out in bands and the
% same page put out whole are the same bytes, which is the requirement
% the mechanism is under and is also why the page cannot answer any of
% this.
/isrec { DEVICE /.playclass known } bind def
/saving { isrec { DEVICE ID /.recordsaving get exec }{ 0 } ifelse } bind def
/cost { isrec { DEVICE ID /.recordcost get exec exch pop exch pop }{ 0 } ifelse } bind def
/band { currentsystemparams /CurBandHeight get } bind def

/W BIG 0 gt { 2550 }{ 2000 } ifelse def
/H BIG 0 gt
    { 3300 }
    { DEVICE /.ncomp get 1 eq { 2010 }{ 676 } ifelse }
  ifelse def

<< /PageSize [W H] >> setpagedevice

% M marks. The large page lays them out as a grid so that every band of
% it carries ink -- a band holding nothing but the ground is a band the
% loop does not paint, and a page whose ink is in one band is a page held
% whole for every purpose here. The small page scatters them for the same
% reason across its two.
/draw {
    gsave 0 setgray
    BIG 0 gt {
        /g M sqrt cvi def
        0 1 g 1 sub {
            /i exch def
            0 1 g 1 sub {
                /j exch def
                i W mul g div 2 add
                j H mul g div 3 add
                2 2 rectfill
            } for
        } for
    }{
        0 1 M 1 sub {
            /i exch def
            i 37 mul W 8 sub mod 3 add
            i 53 mul H 8 sub mod 3 add
            3 3 rectfill
        } for
    } ifelse
    grestore
} bind def

(PAGE ) print W num print ( ) print H num print (\n) print
(ROUTE ) print isrec num print (\n) print
(BAND ) print band num print (\n) print
(SAVING ) print saving num print (\n) print

draw

(COST ) print cost num print (\n) print

showpage
(\nDONE\n) print
flush
